			<!-- Section 1: Why Bridal Sizing is Different -->
			<section style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
				<h3 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 2rem; color: var(--fg-pencil); margin-bottom: 1rem; border-bottom: 2px dashed var(--fg-pencil); padding-bottom: 0.5rem;">
					1. Why Is Bridal Sizing 1 to 2 Sizes Larger Than Street Clothes?
				</h3>
				<p class="font-body" style="line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
					Almost every bride experiences a moment of sticker shock when her bridal stylist takes her measurements and announces a dress size that is two numbers higher than her favorite jeans. Take a deep breath: <strong>this is completely normal and universal across the wedding industry</strong>.
				</p>

				<div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 1.25rem; margin: 1.5rem 0;">
					<div style="background: var(--bg-paper); border: 2px solid var(--fg-pencil); border-radius: 4px; padding: 1.25rem;">
						<h4 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--accent-blue); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
							The "Vanity Sizing" Evolution
						</h4>
						<p class="font-body" style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0;">
							Over the past 50 years, ready-to-wear everyday brands (like Zara, J.Crew, and Nordstrom) shifted measurements upward so shoppers feel flattered buying smaller tag sizes. A modern everyday street Size 6 has roughly the same physical dimensions as a 1960s Size 10.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div style="background: var(--bg-paper); border: 2px solid var(--fg-pencil); border-radius: 4px; padding: 1.25rem;">
						<h4 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--accent-red); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
							European Haute Couture Standards
						</h4>
						<p class="font-body" style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0;">
							Bridal fashion houses never modernized their sizing charts. They still construct gowns using structured, zero-stretch fabrics (silk mikado, dupioni, organza, boned corsets) based on post-WWII European couture size blocks.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
