				<h3 class="font-heading" style="font-size: 2rem; color: var(--fg-pencil); margin-bottom: 1rem; border-bottom: 2px dashed var(--fg-pencil); padding-bottom: 0.5rem;">
					2. How to Measure Yourself with 100% Precision
				</h3>
				<p class="font-body" style="line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
					Never use a metal hardware tape measure—always use a flexible fabric or vinyl measuring tape. Stand upright with feet shoulder-width apart, arms relaxed at your sides:
				</p>

				<div style="background: var(--bg-paper); border: 2px solid var(--fg-pencil); border-radius: 6px; padding: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
					<ul class="font-body" style="line-height: 1.8; font-size: 1.05rem; padding-left: 1.2rem; margin: 0;">
						<li><strong>1. Bust (Fullest Apex):</strong> Wrap the tape horizontally around the fullest part of your bust and straight across your shoulder blades. Wear the exact bra or shapewear you plan to wear beneath your wedding gown.</li>
						<li><strong>2. Natural Waist (Bend to Side):</strong> Bend to one side to find your natural waist crease. This is the narrowest point of your torso, typically located 1 to 2 inches above your belly button. Do not suck in your stomach; breathe normally.</li>
						<li><strong>3. Full Hips (Widest Point):</strong> Stand with your feet touching and wrap the tape around the fullest curve of your hips and buttocks (typically 7 to 9 inches below your natural waistline).</li>
						<li><strong>4. Hollow-to-Hem (Length):</strong> Measure from the hollow depression at the base of your throat down to the floor, wearing the exact bridal heel height you plan to wear on the wedding day.</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</section>

